---
/**
 * Holiday Entitlement Calculator UK — 영국판 제도 도구(basis "law"). 한국어판 연차(/guide/annual-leave-calculator/)와 제도가 달라
 * (근속 가산·1년 미만 월 1일 없음, 5.6주·28일 상한) 짝(hreflang)을 맺지 않는다(조사표 영국 칸 '제도').
 * 계산은 lib/gb/holiday.ts(정수 계산), 화면은 lib/gb/holiday-ui.ts, 숫자는 rates/gb/holiday-entitlement-2026.json
 * (GOV.UK Holiday entitlement updated_at 2026-10-16 · WTR 1998 reg 13·13A·14·15A·15B · WTR (NI) 2016 reg 15·16·17·19 — 2026-10-21 확인).
 *
 * 소제목은 영국 구글 자동완성에서 왔다(keywords/gb-holiday-entitlement-2026-10-21.csv, hl en · gl gb):
 *   holiday entitlement calculator uk · pro rata · part time · in hours · starting mid year · when leaving a job · northern ireland /
 *   holiday pay calculator 12.07 · irregular hours / annual leave calculator hours to days · for leavers
 * 계산하지 않는 것(rates notCalculated): 휴가 급여 금액, 북아일랜드 irregular hours, irregular 근로자의 퇴사 보상·28일 시간 환산,
 * leave year 중간 입사의 날짜 비례, 통지 기간, 누가 irregular-hours 근로자인지 판정.
 */
import BaseHead from "../../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../../components/AdSlot.astro";
import ClarityHead from "../../../components/ClarityHead.astro";
import GtagHead from "../../../components/GtagHead.astro";
import TrackLinks from "../../../components/TrackLinks.astro";
import AdSenseHead from "../../../components/AdSenseHead.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import { annualDays, annualHours, firstYearAccrued, leaving, irregularAccrued, fmt } from "../../../lib/gb/holiday";
import "../../../styles/global.css";

const title = "Holiday Entitlement Calculator UK — Statutory Annual Leave in Days and Hours";
const description =
  "Work out UK statutory holiday: 5.6 weeks capped at 28 days, part-time pro rata, first-year accrual, leave owed when leaving and 12.07% for irregular hours.";

const five = annualDays(5);
const three = annualDays(3);
const four = annualDays(4);
const six = annualDays(6);
const h225 = annualHours(3, 22.5)!;
const h375 = annualHours(5, 37.5)!;
const fy1 = firstYearAccrued(5, 1);
const fy3 = firstYearAccrued(5, 3);
const fy4 = firstYearAccrued(5, 4);
const fy3d = firstYearAccrued(3, 1);
const lv = leaving(5, "2026-01-01", "2026-07-01", 10)!;
const ir30 = irregularAccrued(30);
const ir29 = irregularAccrued(29);
const ir25 = irregularAccrued(25);
---

<html lang="en-GB">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <ClarityHead />
    <GtagHead />
    <AdSenseHead />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-texture">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <h1>Holiday Entitlement Calculator UK</h1>
      <p>
        Almost all workers in the UK are legally entitled to 5.6 weeks' paid holiday a year — 28 days for someone who works 5 days a
        week. Type how many days a week you work to see your statutory minimum. Further down you can work out leave in your first year,
        holiday left when you leave a job, and leave built up by irregular-hours workers. The calculator counts days and hours only; it
        does not work out holiday pay.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="he-days">Days worked a week</label>
          <input type="number" id="he-days" value="5" min="0" max="7" step="0.5" inputmode="decimal" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="he-hours">Hours worked a week (optional)</label>
          <input type="number" id="he-hours" value="" min="0" step="0.5" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="e.g. 37.5" />
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-dock="he-value">
          <p class="label">Statutory paid holiday a year</p>
          <p class="big" id="he-value">{fmt(five.days)} days</p>
          <p class="hint" id="he-formula">5 days a week × 5.6 weeks = {fmt(five.days)} days</p>
        </div>

        <div class="stat-row">
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">In weeks</span>
            <span class="value" id="he-weeks">5.6 weeks</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">In hours</span>
            <span class="value" id="he-in-hours">—</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-download" type="button">Save as image</button>
          <button id="btn-share" type="button" class="secondary">Share</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>How is holiday entitlement calculated in the UK?</h2>
      <p>
        The statutory minimum is 5.6 weeks of paid leave each leave year: 4 weeks under regulation 13 of the Working Time Regulations
        1998 and another 1.6 weeks under regulation 13A. In days, multiply the days you work each week by 5.6. The total is capped at
        28 days, so working more days a week does not take you past 28.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>5 days a week: 5 × 5.6 = {fmt(five.days)} days</li>
        <li>4 days a week: 4 × 5.6 = {fmt(four.days)} days</li>
        <li>3 days a week: 3 × 5.6 = {fmt(three.days)} days (the example GOV.UK gives)</li>
        <li>6 days a week: 6 × 5.6 = {fmt(six.uncapped)}, but the cap means {fmt(six.days)} days</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        An employer can include bank holidays in the 28 days — they do not have to be given on top. An employer can also offer more
        leave than the legal minimum; this calculator only shows the statutory minimum, so check your contract for anything extra.
      </p>

      <h2>Part-time holiday entitlement (pro rata)</h2>
      <p>
        Part-time workers on regular hours for the whole year get the same 5.6 weeks, which comes to fewer than 28 days. That is what
        "pro rata" holiday means here: the 5.6 weeks are measured in your own working weeks. If you work half days, enter them as halves
        — 2.5 days a week gives {fmt(annualDays(2.5).days)} days.
      </p>

      <h2>Holiday entitlement in hours</h2>
      <p>
        If your employer counts leave in hours, 5.6 weeks of your usual weekly hours gives the same entitlement: 22.5 hours a week ×
        5.6 = {fmt(h225)} hours, and 37.5 hours a week × 5.6 = {fmt(h375)} hours. Enter your weekly hours above to see this. The
        calculator only converts to hours when you work 5 days a week or fewer — above that the 28-day cap applies, and turning 28 days
        into hours depends on how long your working days are, which the regulations do not fix.
      </p>

      <h2 id="first-year">Holiday in your first year (starting mid-year)</h2>
      <p>
        In the first year of a job, regulation 15A says leave builds up at one-twelfth of the yearly amount on the first day of each
        month. A fraction of a day that is not a half day is rounded up: below a half day becomes a half day, above it becomes a whole
        day. GOV.UK's examples for a 5-day week: {fmt(fy1)} days for the first month (28 ÷ 12 = 2.33) and {fmt(fy3)} days after three
        months (28 ÷ 12 × 3). After four months it is {fmt(fy4)} days, and a 3-day week earns {fmt(fy3d)} days in the first month.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="fy-days">Days worked a week</label>
          <input type="number" id="fy-days" value="5" min="0" max="7" step="0.5" inputmode="decimal" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="fy-months">Months started in your first year (including this one)</label>
          <input type="number" id="fy-months" value="3" min="1" max="12" step="1" inputmode="numeric" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="fy-taken">Days already taken</label>
          <input type="number" id="fy-taken" value="0" min="0" step="0.5" inputmode="decimal" />
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
          <p class="label">Leave built up so far</p>
          <p class="big" id="fy-value">{fmt(fy3)} days</p>
          <p class="hint" id="fy-formula">28 ÷ 12 × 3 = 7, rounded up to the next half day</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-row">
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Left to take now</span>
            <span class="value" id="fy-left">{fmt(fy3)} days</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        GOV.UK adds that an employer can instead work out the first month "pro rata", by the number of days worked, in which case the
        one-twelfth is not given from the first day. This calculator uses the one-twelfth method. Regulation 15A does not apply to
        anyone whose job began on or before 25 October 2001.
      </p>

      <h2 id="leaving">Holiday entitlement when leaving a job</h2>
      <p>
        When you leave part way through a leave year, regulation 14 sets the untaken leave you must be paid for, unless your contract or
        another agreement says otherwise: (A × B) − C, where A is your yearly statutory leave, B is the share of the leave year that has
        passed before your leaving date, and C is the leave you have already taken that year. Payment in place of statutory leave is
        only allowed when you leave, and GOV.UK says it is due even after a dismissal for gross misconduct.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="lv-days">Days worked a week</label>
          <input type="number" id="lv-days" value="5" min="0" max="7" step="0.5" inputmode="decimal" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="lv-start">First day of the leave year</label>
          <input type="date" id="lv-start" value="2026-01-01" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="lv-end">Leaving date</label>
          <input type="date" id="lv-end" value="2026-07-01" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="lv-taken">Days taken this leave year</label>
          <input type="number" id="lv-taken" value="10" min="0" step="0.5" inputmode="decimal" />
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
          <p class="label">Untaken statutory leave</p>
          <p class="big" id="lv-value">{fmt(lv.owed)} days</p>
          <p class="hint" id="lv-formula">({fmt(lv.a)} × {lv.elapsedDays}/{lv.yearDays}) − 10 = {fmt(lv.owed)}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-row">
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">A — leave for the year</span>
            <span class="value" id="lv-a">{fmt(lv.a)} days</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">B — leave year passed</span>
            <span class="value" id="lv-b">{lv.elapsedDays} of {lv.yearDays} days ({fmt(lv.b * 100, 1)}%)</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        For B, this page counts the days from the first day of the leave year up to the day before your leaving date, and divides by
        the number of days in that leave year (366 if it includes 29 February). That is this page's setting — the regulation only says
        "the proportion of the worker's leave year which expired before the termination date". The result is not rounded, because
        regulation 14 has no rounding rule. If you have taken more than your share, GOV.UK says the employer must not take money from your
        final pay unless that was agreed in writing beforehand.
      </p>

      <h2 id="irregular">Irregular hours and part-year workers: the 12.07% rule</h2>
      <p>
        In England, Scotland and Wales, irregular-hours and part-year workers build up leave at 12.07% of the hours they work in each pay
        period, for leave years starting on or after 1 April 2024 (regulation 15B). The leave is added on the last day of the pay period,
        and a part-hour under 30 minutes counts as nothing while 30 minutes or more counts as a whole hour. GOV.UK's example: 30 hours in
        a weekly pay period × 12.07% = {fmt(ir30.raw, 3)}, so {ir30.hours} hours of leave. 29 hours gives {fmt(ir29.raw, 4)} →{" "}
        {ir29.hours} hours, and 25 hours gives {fmt(ir25.raw, 4)} → {ir25.hours} hours.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ir-hours">Hours worked in the pay period</label>
          <input type="number" id="ir-hours" value="30" min="0" step="0.25" inputmode="decimal" />
        </div>
        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
          <p class="label">Leave built up this pay period</p>
          <p class="big" id="ir-value">{ir30.hours} hours</p>
          <p class="hint" id="ir-formula">30 hours × 12.07% = 3.621 hours, rounded to the nearest hour</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        GOV.UK describes irregular hours as work where the number of hours in a pay period often or always changes, and part-year work as
        having periods of at least a week in a leave year without work or pay. A worker cannot build up more than 28 days in a leave year
        this way. For leave years that began on or before 31 March 2024 the older rules apply instead. This calculator does not decide
        whether you are an irregular-hours worker, and it does not cover Northern Ireland, whose 2016 regulations we did not find a 12.07%
        rule in.
      </p>

      <h2>Northern Ireland</h2>
      <p>
        Northern Ireland has its own Working Time Regulations (Northern Ireland) 2016. The figures used in the first three calculators are
        the same there: 4 weeks plus 1.6 weeks, a 28-day cap, one-twelfth a month in the first year with the same half-day rounding, and
        the same (A × B) − C formula when leaving.
      </p>

      <h2>Carrying over leave</h2>
      <p>
        GOV.UK says a worker who gets 28 days can carry over at most 8 days to the next year, if the contract allows it. Leave missed
        because of maternity, paternity or other family leave can be carried over. Leave missed through sickness can be carried over up to
        20 of the 28 days for regular-hours workers, or up to 28 days for irregular-hours and part-year workers. In England, Scotland and
        Wales, regulation 13 says sick-leave carry-over must be used within 18 months of the end of the leave year it came from.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Does this calculator work out holiday pay?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Workers are entitled to a week's pay for each week of statutory leave, and how a week's pay is worked out depends on the
          working pattern — see the holiday pay section of GOV.UK's holiday entitlement guide.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Are bank holidays on top of 28 days?</summary>
        <p>
          Not by law. GOV.UK says bank or public holidays do not have to be given as paid leave, and an employer can count them as part of
          statutory annual leave. Your contract may give them in addition.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>When does my leave year start?</summary>
        <p>
          Your contract should say. If it does not, regulation 13 says the leave year starts on the day your job began (if you started
          after 1 October 1998) and on each anniversary of that date.
        </p>
      </details>

      <p>
        Related: <a href="/en-gb/tools/date-diff/">date calculator</a> for counting days between two dates, and the
        <a href="/en-gb/tools/sec-convert/">decimal hours converter</a> for timesheets.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.gov.uk/holiday-entitlement-rights" rel="noopener" target="_blank">GOV.UK — Holiday entitlement</a> (updated 16
          October 2026, checked 21 October 2026)
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/1833/contents" rel="noopener" target="_blank">The Working Time Regulations 1998</a>,
          regulations 13, 13A, 14, 15A and 15B (checked 21 October 2026)
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisr/2016/49/contents" rel="noopener" target="_blank"
            >The Working Time Regulations (Northern Ireland) 2016</a
          >, regulations 15, 16, 17 and 19 (checked 21 October 2026)
        </li>
      </ul>

      <AdSlot label="holiday-result-en-gb" />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
    <script src="../../../lib/gb/holiday-ui.ts"></script>
    <TrackLinks />
    <script src="../../../scripts/calc-ui.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
